var e=1,t=`der-hedging-01`,n=`derivatives`,r=`Hedging in Practice: Basis Risk and Why Hedges Fail`,i=`An 85%-correlated hedge removes 72% of the variance and leaves 53% of the volatility — and hedges fail on cash, not on correlation.`,a=`advanced`,o=[`der-swaps-01`,`der-vol-01`],s=9,c=[`CAIA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`A hedge takes an offsetting position so that a loss on one side is compensated by a gain on the other. In principle it removes a risk. In practice it **exchanges** one risk for a smaller and different one, and the differences have names.

**[[basis-risk|Basis risk]].** The hedge instrument is not the exposure. A different grade, a different location, a different maturity, a different index. The gap between them is left unhedged and it widens under stress.

**Quantity risk.** The exposure changes size and the hedge does not. A hedge sized to a forecast is wrong whenever the forecast is.

**Timing risk.** The hedge settles on a date; the exposure does not.

**Liquidity risk.** The hedge demands cash before the exposure delivers it — the single most common way a correct hedge produces a failure.

**So a hedge is never free and never complete.** The right question is not "is this hedged?" but **"what is left, how big is it, and what would make it worse?"** Those three have numbers, and most hedging discussions never reach them.`},{type:`concept`,id:`correlation-is-not-enough`,body:`**A hedge that is 85% correlated with your exposure sounds close to complete, and it removes only about half the [[volatility]].**

The arithmetic is unforgiving because variance is squared. Hedging an asset with a proxy at correlation ρ leaves residual volatility of **σ × √(1 − ρ²)**:

- **ρ = 0.95** → residual **31%** of the original volatility
- **ρ = 0.85** → residual **53%**
- **ρ = 0.70** → residual **71%**
- **ρ = 0.50** → residual **87%**

**At 85% correlation — which most people would describe as a good hedge — you still hold more than half the volatility you started with.**

Two things follow, and the second is the more dangerous.

**Proxy hedges are much weaker than they feel.** Hedging a private portfolio with a listed index, or a specific credit with a broad index, leaves most of the risk.

**Correlations fall precisely when hedges are needed.** The 85% measured in calm markets is not the 85% that applies in a dislocation, when idiosyncratic moves dominate and relationships break. **The hedge is weakest at the moment it is being relied on**, which is the opposite of what a risk report computed on historical correlation will suggest.`},{type:`intuition`,id:`hedges-fail-on-cash`,body:`**Almost every hedging failure worth studying was a hedge that worked. The losses came from cash timing, not from the economics.**

The pattern repeats across decades and markets:

**Metallgesellschaft, 1993.** Long-dated supply contracts hedged with short-dated futures rolled forward. The economics were sound over the contract lives; the futures required margin as prices moved, and the positions were liquidated before the hedge could deliver.

**UK pensions, 2022.** Liability hedges via swaps and repo. Rates rose sharply, the hedges lost value and demanded collateral immediately, while the offsetting liability reduction was an actuarial figure spread over decades.

**Any currency hedge on an illiquid asset.** The currency moves favourably for the asset, the hedge loses, and cash is required against a gain that is only a quarterly mark.

**The common structure: the hedge settles in cash on a short cycle, and the thing it hedges settles slowly or not at all.**

So the discipline is a cash-flow one rather than a statistical one. **Before putting on any hedge, write down two columns: when the hedge pays or demands cash, and when the exposure does.** If those columns do not match, the position needs a liquidity facility sized to a plausible adverse move — and that sizing is the actual hedging decision.

**A hedge you cannot fund is not a hedge. It is a position that will be closed at the worst possible moment.**`},{type:`example`,id:`worked-basis`,body:`A portfolio with 20% annualised volatility is hedged with an instrument whose volatility is 18% and whose correlation with the portfolio is 85%.`,walkthrough:[`**Optimal hedge ratio** = ρ × σ_asset / σ_hedge = 0.85 × 20 / 18 = **0.944**. Hedge roughly 94% of the notional, not 100%.`,`**Residual volatility** = 20% × √(1 − 0.85²) = 20% × 0.527 = **10.54%**.`,`**Variance removed** = 1 − (1 − 0.85²) = **72%**. Impressive on variance.`,`**Volatility remaining** = 10.54 / 20 = **53%** of the original. Much less impressive on volatility.`,`**Read that carefully: the same hedge removes 72% of the variance and leaves 53% of the volatility.** Which figure you quote determines whether the hedge sounds excellent or mediocre, and both are correct.`,`Now degrade the correlation to 70%, which is what happens when relationships break in a dislocation: residual volatility rises to **14.28%**, or 71% of the original.`,`**So a hedge that was removing half the risk is now removing under a third — and the deterioration happens exactly when the hedge matters.**`,`Sanity check worth reusing: **compute √(1 − ρ²) before accepting any proxy hedge.** It converts a comfortable-sounding correlation into the fraction of risk still held, and the answer is nearly always larger than expected.`]},{type:`table`,id:`basis-table`,caption:`Where basis risk comes from`,headers:[`Mismatch`,`Example`,`When it bites`],rows:[[`Instrument`,`Hedging a specific credit with an index`,`Idiosyncratic events — exactly what you were worried about.`],[`Maturity`,`Short futures rolled against a long exposure`,`When the curve shape moves against the roll.`],[`Location or grade`,`Hedging a regional commodity with a benchmark contract`,`Transport disruption, local shortage, quality spreads.`],[`Timing`,`Daily-settling hedge, quarterly-marked exposure`,`Immediately, in cash — the most common failure.`],[`Quantity`,`Hedge sized to forecast volumes`,`Whenever the forecast is wrong, in either direction.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Over-hedging.** Hedging 100% of a notional when the optimal ratio is 94% creates a small opposite position. It looks conservative and it is a position.

**Hedge ratios set once.** The optimal ratio depends on volatilities and correlation, both of which move. A ratio from two years ago is a stale estimate.

**Ignoring the hedge's own basis.** A currency forward hedging a private asset whose value is a quarterly estimate is hedging an estimate, not a value.

**Hedging the measured risk rather than the real one.** Hedging equity beta while the portfolio's actual exposure is to [[credit-spread|credit spreads]] and liquidity leaves the real risk untouched and adds cost.

**Accounting mismatch.** A hedge marked to market against an exposure held at cost produces reported volatility from a position designed to reduce it — and the pressure to remove the hedge comes from the reporting rather than from the economics.

**Forgetting that the hedge has a counterparty.** In the scenario where your hedge pays out most, your counterparty is losing most. Concentration matters exactly then.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`should-you-hedge`,body:`**The decision to hedge is not primarily a view about the risk. It is a question about what you cannot afford.**

Hedging costs money — premium, carry, basis, operational complexity — and it removes upside along with downside. An investor with a long horizon, no forced sales and no reporting constraints is frequently better off unhedged, accepting the volatility and keeping the cost.

So three questions decide it, and none of them is about forecasting:

**Would this loss force an action?** Breaching a [[covenant]], failing a funding test, triggering redemptions, forcing an asset sale. If yes, hedge — the cost buys the avoidance of a forced decision, which is the most expensive kind.

**Is the risk one you are paid to take?** An alternatives portfolio is paid for illiquidity and credit risk. It is not paid for currency exposure, and unpaid risks are the first candidates for hedging.

**Can you fund the hedge in its bad scenario?** If not, the hedge is a liability rather than a protection.

**The answer that follows is usually: hedge the unpaid risks that could force an action, at a size you can fund, and accept the rest.** That is a narrower programme than most institutions run, and it is considerably more likely to survive the event it was built for.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Compute √(1 − ρ²) for any proxy hedge.** It converts a correlation into the share of risk you are still carrying, and the number is sobering.

**Write the two cash-flow columns before putting on any hedge** — when the hedge settles, when the exposure settles. Mismatches need a funded liquidity line.

**Ask what the hedge costs per year in carry, premium and expected basis.** A hedge with no stated cost has an unmeasured one.

**Ask which risks the portfolio is paid to take, and hedge only the others.** Currency and rates are usually unpaid; illiquidity and credit usually are paid.

**Stress the correlation, not just the market.** Re-run the hedge's effectiveness at a correlation 15 points lower — that is the version that applies in the event you are hedging.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`A hedge exchanges one risk for a smaller different one. The right question is what remains, how large it is, and what makes it worse.`,`Residual volatility is σ × √(1 − ρ²): at 85% correlation a hedge removes 72% of the variance and leaves 53% of the volatility.`,`Hedges fail on cash timing rather than on economics — Metallgesellschaft and the 2022 UK pension episode were both correct hedges with unfundable collateral calls.`,`Hedge the unpaid risks that could force an action, at a size you can fund, and accept the rest. That is a narrower programme than most institutions run.`]}],u=[{id:`der-hedging-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`hedging`,`definition`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`What does a hedge actually do?`,choices:[`Removes a risk completely`,`Exchanges one risk for a smaller and different one — leaving basis, timing, quantity and liquidity risk behind`,`Guarantees a profit if the market moves against you`,`Eliminates the need to hold capital against the position`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Complete removal would require an instrument identical to the exposure, settling on the same dates, in the same size. That is rare enough to treat as the exception.`,`Correct. What remains has names — basis, timing, quantity, liquidity — and each has a size that can be estimated before the hedge is put on.`,`It offsets a loss with a gain; it does not create a profit, and the offset is imperfect.`,`Hedges frequently increase near-term capital requirements through margin and collateral.`],explanation:`Framing the question as "what is left?" rather than "is it hedged?" is the whole discipline. The residual has a magnitude, and the useful hedging conversations are the ones that reach it.`},{id:`der-hedging-01-q2`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`hedging`,`basis`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-basis`,stem:`A portfolio has 20% volatility and is hedged with an instrument correlated 0.85 with it. What is the residual volatility, in percent? Two decimal places.`,answer:10.54,tolerance:.2,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`%`,inputHint:`σ × √(1 − ρ²)`,explanation:`More than half the original volatility survives a hedge most people would describe as good. This one calculation reframes proxy hedging more effectively than any amount of discussion about correlation levels.`},{id:`der-hedging-01-q3`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`hedging`,`hedge-ratio`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-basis`,stem:`Asset volatility 20%, hedge instrument volatility 18%, correlation 0.85. What is the optimal hedge ratio? Two decimal places.`,answer:.94,tolerance:.03,toleranceType:`abs`,inputHint:`ρ × σ_asset / σ_hedge`,explanation:`Not 1.00 — hedging the full notional would over-hedge, because the instrument is more volatile per unit than the correlation justifies. Over-hedging feels prudent and creates an opposite position nobody intended.`},{id:`der-hedging-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`hedging`,`correlation`],concept:`correlation-is-not-enough`,stem:`Why does a hedge with 85% correlation leave so much risk?`,choices:[`Because correlations are measured with error`,`Because residual volatility scales with √(1 − ρ²), which at ρ = 0.85 is 0.53 — so more than half the volatility remains`,`Because hedge instruments have transaction costs`,`Because 85% correlation is unusually low for a hedge`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Estimation error is a real additional problem and not the arithmetic reason.`,`Correct. Variance reduction goes with ρ², so 72% of variance is removed — but volatility is the square root, and 53% of it survives.`,`Costs reduce the return and do not create residual risk.`,`85% is a good correlation for a proxy hedge, which is exactly what makes the result surprising.`],explanation:`The gap between the variance figure and the volatility figure is why hedging conversations often talk past each other: a risk manager quoting 72% variance reduction and a portfolio manager seeing half the swings are both describing the same hedge accurately.`},{id:`der-hedging-01-q5`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`hedging`,`misconception`],concept:`hedges-fail-on-cash`,stem:`"The hedge lost a lot of money last quarter, so it was a bad hedge." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — a hedge losing money means the thing it protects gained, which is the hedge working; the real test is whether the two legs offset and whether the cash calls were fundable`,`False — hedges should never lose money`,`True — a losing position is evidence of a poor decision`,`False — because hedges are not marked to market`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. The derivative's loss is half of an offsetting pair. Judging it alone guarantees the wrong conclusion, and it is how working hedges get removed just before they are needed.`,`A hedge that never loses is not hedging anything — it would be a position with only upside.`,`The loss is expected in exactly the scenario the hedge was bought for.`,`Hedges typically are marked to market, and that visibility is part of the reporting problem.`],explanation:`The reporting mismatch is the practical danger: a hedge marked to market against an exposure held at cost creates visible losses against invisible gains, and the pressure to remove it comes from the presentation rather than from the economics.`},{id:`der-hedging-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`hedging`,`liquidity`,`on-the-job`],concept:`hedges-fail-on-cash`,stem:`What do the Metallgesellschaft losses and the 2022 UK pension crisis have in common?`,choices:[`Both involved hedges that were economically wrong`,`Both involved economically sound hedges whose cash collateral demands arrived far faster than the offsetting benefit`,`Both involved fraud`,`Both were caused by counterparty default`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`In both cases the hedge would have worked over the exposure's life. That is what makes them instructive rather than simply cautionary.`,`Correct. Short-cycle cash settlement against slow-settling exposures. The positions were closed under funding pressure before the economics could be realised.`,`Neither episode was primarily a fraud.`,`Counterparties performed; the problem was the hedger's own liquidity.`],explanation:`Both are the same lesson thirty years apart, which suggests it is structural rather than a matter of competence. The practical response is a mapped cash-flow timeline for every hedge and a funded liquidity facility sized to a plausible adverse move.`},{id:`der-hedging-01-q7`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`hedging`,`policy`,`on-the-job`],concept:`should-you-hedge`,stem:`Which risk in an alternatives portfolio is the strongest candidate for hedging?`,choices:[`Illiquidity, since it is uncomfortable`,`Currency exposure, because it is a risk the portfolio is not paid to take`,`Credit risk, because defaults are unpredictable`,`Manager selection risk`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Illiquidity is a risk the portfolio is deliberately paid for through the illiquidity premium — hedging it away would remove the return it was allocated for.`,`Correct. Currency adds volatility without an expected return for a diversified investor, so it is the classic unpaid risk and the first candidate.`,`Credit is also a paid risk; it is the reason a credit allocation exists.`,`Real and not hedgeable with an instrument — it is managed through diligence and diversification.`],explanation:`The paid-versus-unpaid framing does most of the work in setting hedging policy. It also explains why currency hedging policy is one of the few genuinely settled questions in institutional investing, while credit and illiquidity hedging mostly is not.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
